I figure the number would change by a thousand every second if the
robot wasn't so slow. I know! Let's put the robot that's doing all the work
on the back of the number. Then when we flip it back over the robot will start
working on the back.  And he should be fast enough.
You can flip what you are holding by pressing the 'f' button.

We need a number that
keeps getting bigger
and has a robot on the
other side.

This is the result of saving everything in a room.
4!@%!(%@*ATAnATAlAkA0A0A5A D..

   The following is a blank number pad.
4!@%!(%@!C7C!C!C!C+A"C..

NumberTyping = off
TextTyping = off
FunctionKeys = off
HandEmptyAfterPosting = off
BringInVacuumWhenRobotFails = off
UserPuzzle = off

Did you click on the 'f' button while holding the number?

Did you put the robot on the back of the number?

Did you give the robot the box to work on?

Did you pick up the number and press 'f' to flip it back over?

Did you set the number down to start it working?

Well, what I would do is press 'f' while still holding
the number. Then I'd drop the robot on the back. Then I'd drop
the box on the robot. I'd pick up the number and press 'f' to flip
it back over. I'd set it down on the floor to be sure it is working
ok. Then I'd bring back the number.
